etymologies.text:
From East Anglian dialectal English cliver (English “expert at seizing”), from Middle English cliver (“tenacious”).

* perhaps from Old English *clifer, clibbor (Old English “clinging”);
* or perhaps from Dutch —, Low German —, or East/Saterland Frisian — (compare kluftich (Saterland Frisian “clever, prudent”), probably derived from Proto-West Germanic *kleuban (“to cleave, split”));
* or dialectal Norwegian klover (“ready, skillful”), itself borrowed from Middle Low German klever, related to kleven (Middle Low German “to stick”), from Old Saxon klibōn, from Proto-West Germanic *klibēn, related to the Old English word above;
* possibly influenced by clifer (Old English “claw, hand”) (compare clawian (Old English “to claw”)).

Related to cleave (English). Perhaps influenced by celfydd (Welsh “talented, dexterous, expert”).

Compare typologically chytrý (Czech), хи́трый (Russian) (akin to хвата́ть (Russian)), also note схва́тывать на лету́ (Russian).
